<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lindisfarne Gospels</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> is an illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the four Gospels made at the monastery of Lindisfarne in Northumbria around 700–715, traditionally by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Eadfrith. Its decorated carpet pages and initials combine Irish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> styles. In the 10th century a priest named Aldred added an Old English gloss, the oldest surviving translation of the Gospels into English. It is held by the British Library.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lindisfarne-gospels.jpg" alt="Lindisfarne Gospels" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eadfrith of Lindisfarne (presumed) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LindisfarneFol27rIncipitMatt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Lindisfarne Gospels were produced in the monastery at Lindisfarne, off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>, and are presumed to be the work of a monk named Eadfrith, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Lindisfarne in 698 and died in 721. The book was made in honour of God and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-cuthbert">Saint Cuthbert</a>, who died on 20 March 687, and some parts were left unfinished, so Eadfrith was probably still working on it when he died.</p><p>In the 10th century Aldred, a priest of the monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester-le-street">Chester-le-Street</a>, recorded in a colophon four men who made the book: Eadfrith wrote it, Ethelwald, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> islanders, bound it, the anchorite Billfrith ornamented it, and Aldred himself glossed it. The work took about five to ten years and used roughly 150 calf skins for its 516 pages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> contains the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. It opens with a carpet page in the form of a cross and a major initial page for the letter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Saint Jerome</a> to Pope Damasus I, followed by sixteen pages of canon tables. A portrait of the Evangelist, a carpet page and a decorated initial page precede each Gospel. Following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, each Evangelist has his own symbol: Matthew the man, Mark the lion, Luke the calf and John the eagle.</p><p>The art is Insular, combining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> and Celtic elements, with interlaced animals and birds, spirals and knotwork. Eadfrith made 90 of his own colours from six local minerals and vegetable extracts. The blue, long thought to be ultramarine from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, was found by Raman microscopy in the 2000s to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Old English gloss that Aldred inserted between the lines about 250 years after the book was made is the oldest extant translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> into English. Because of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids the monastic community left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> around 875, taking Cuthbert's body and its books, and settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester-le-street">Chester-le-Street</a>.</p><p>The original jewelled binding was lost, and a new one was made in 1852 by Smith, Nicholson and Co. for Bishop Edward Maltby. After the Dissolution of the Monasteries the book passed to Sir Robert Cotton in the early 17th century, and in 1753 it became part of the founding collections of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. It is now in the British Library. When it was displayed at Palace Green Library in Durham in 2013, nearly 100,000 visitors saw it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lindisfarne Gospels” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Lindisfarne Gospels</b>, made around 700, and the Codex Amiatinus, the oldest surviving complete Latin Bible, taken towards Rome by Abbot Ceolfrith in 716, are among the finest books of the early Middle Ages.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
